<section class="cg-module" aria-labelledby="study-title" data-study-progression><div class="cg-module__heading"><div><p class="cg-kicker">Five experiments</p><h2 id="study-title">How the five studies built on each other</h2></div></div><ol class="cg-study-list">
<li><article class="cg-study" data-study-card><span class="cg-study__number">01</span><div><h3>Experiment 1: Does display change achievability?</h3><p>Line-graph presentation increased retirement-goal achievability.</p></div></article></li>
<li><article class="cg-study" data-study-card><span class="cg-study__number">02</span><div><h3>Experiment 2: Does it change planned contributions?</h3><p>Line graphs increased both retirement-goal achievability and planned monthly retirement contributions, with achievability statistically mediating the contribution effect.</p></div></article></li>
<li><article class="cg-study" data-study-card><span class="cg-study__number">03</span><div><h3>Experiment 3: Which confidence changes?</h3><p>Line graphs increased growth confidence rather than ability confidence, with growth confidence statistically mediating the effect on retirement-goal achievability.</p></div></article></li>
<li><article class="cg-study" data-study-card><span class="cg-study__number">04</span><div><h3>Experiment 4: Does perceived acceleration help explain it?</h3><p>Line graphs increased perceived compound-growth acceleration, and the results were statistically consistent with the proposed pathway.</p></div></article></li>
<li><article class="cg-study" data-study-card><span class="cg-study__number">05</span><div><h3>Experiment 5: What part of the visual representation matters?</h3><p>The line graph again exceeded the table on perceived acceleration, but the area-card condition left the exact visual mechanism unresolved.</p></div></article></li>
</ol></section>

<section class="cg-module" aria-labelledby="experiment-four-context-title"><div class="cg-module__heading"><div><p class="cg-kicker">Experiment 4</p><h2 id="experiment-four-context-title">Measuring perceived compound-growth acceleration</h2></div></div><div class="cg-two-column"><div><p>Experiment 4 randomly assigned participants aged 24–30 to see the same $100-per-month retirement projection as either a numerical table or line graph. The study added the Estimated Growth Ratio, a measure designed to capture whether participants perceived the later increase in growth as larger than the earlier increase.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="cg-module" aria-labelledby="egr-title" data-egr-explainer><div class="cg-module__heading"><div><p class="cg-kicker">Measure</p><h2 id="egr-title">How perceived acceleration was measured</h2></div></div><p class="cg-egr-intro">The Estimated Growth Ratio compares the later increase in perceived growth with the earlier increase.</p><div class="cg-switch" aria-label="Estimated Growth Ratio example"><button type="button" data-egr-preset="equal-gains" aria-pressed="false">Equal gains</button><button type="button" data-egr-preset="compound" data-egr-authority-preset="experimental-projection" aria-pressed="true">Experimental projection</button></div><div class="cg-egr"><div class="cg-egr__points" aria-label="Growth estimates"><div><span>Year 20</span><strong data-egr-year20>3.6×</strong></div><div><span>Year 30</span><strong data-egr-year30>10.3×</strong></div><div><span>Year 40</span><strong data-egr-year40>27.8×</strong></div></div><div class="cg-egr__gains"><div class="cg-egr__gain"><span>Earlier gain</span><strong data-egr-earlier>10.3 − 3.6 = 6.7</strong></div><div class="cg-egr__division" aria-hidden="true">÷</div><div class="cg-egr__gain"><span>Later gain</span><strong data-egr-later>27.8 − 10.3 = 17.5</strong></div></div><div class="cg-egr__result" aria-live="polite"><span>Estimated Growth Ratio</span><strong data-egr-ratio>2.61</strong><p data-egr-interpretation>2.61 = the correct ratio for the experimental projection.</p></div></div><p class="cg-persistent-note">Values above 1 indicate that the later gain is perceived as larger than the earlier gain.</p></section>

<section class="cg-module" aria-labelledby="results-title" data-experiment-four><div class="cg-module__heading"><div><p class="cg-kicker">Experiment 4 results</p><h2 id="results-title">What changed?</h2></div></div><div class="cg-result-list" id="result-list"></div><div class="cg-result-detail" id="experiment-four-detail" aria-live="polite">Focus or tap a condition marker for its mean, standard deviation, and comparison statistic.</div>
</section>

<section class="cg-module" aria-labelledby="implications-title"><div class="cg-module__heading"><div><p class="cg-kicker">Implications</p><h2 id="implications-title">What this could mean for retirement tools</h2></div></div><div class="cg-implications"><article><h3>Exact values</h3><p>Tables are useful when someone needs to find or compare precise values.</p></article><article><h3>Growth patterns</h3><p>Line graphs can make changes in growth over time more apparent.</p></article><article><h3>Using both</h3><p>Showing exact values and a visual trajectory may support different tasks better than treating the formats as interchangeable.</p></article></div></section>
